{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B022",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B007"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B022 recognizes a sharply bounded advance in experimental physics: magic-wavelength confinement made it possible to interrogate many atoms with control of a central systematic limitation, and the resulting optical lattice clocks have become an independently reproduced, exceptionally precise measurement platform. Katori's defining concept and Ye's decisive experimental and metrological development form a credible two-person core. The mature achievement is not a prospective application but the demonstrated ability to make time, frequency, gravity, and related quantities accessible at a qualitatively new precision. This is precisely the kind of reliable new observational capability whose physical uses can expand over time.",
    "case_against": "The citation must not claim the whole optical-clock enterprise or credit every later performance improvement. The documentary record still needs to establish the exact boundary between Katori's original magic-wavelength concept, Ye's essential development, and broader enabling work. Nor should we present developing fundamental-physics applications as if they were already settled headline discoveries. These qualifications narrow the claim but do not weaken the mature experimental achievement itself.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson prefers inflation because of its foundational explanatory reach, while acknowledging that no distinctive observation uniquely selects inflation over every viable alternative.",
        "response": "I agree that B033 is a profound and observationally constrained framework. For this decision, however, the absence of a discriminating signature remains a material limitation when compared with B022's directly repeatable experimental performance. A prize for lattice clocks would recognize an established physical measurement regime, not merely improved technology, and its attribution is more compact than the broader theoretical history behind inflation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that spherical-aberration correction transformed TEM into an atom-resolving quantitative instrument, but calls for reconstruction of the respective contributions of Rose, Haider, Urban, other contributors, and later development.",
        "response": "I share Olsson's high assessment of what aberration correction made observable; it is an excellent example of instrumentation transforming physics. Her attribution reservation is nevertheless decisive at this stage. The same need for careful history applies to B022, but the proposed Katori-Ye division is presently more readily delimited as concept and defining experimental development. B061 should remain a strong alternative pending a more exact reconstruction of the original practical breakthrough."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors conformal field theory for its enduring foundational influence and coherent attribution, while noting its less immediate relation to directly measurable systems.",
        "response": "B027 has exceptional theoretical depth and a coherent formulation. I give B022 priority because its physical principle has already been converted into reproducible instruments that extend what can be measured across laboratories. This is not an argument from downstream popularity: the decisive point is the experimentally demonstrated and durable new measurement capability, with a comparably bounded attribution once the historical record is checked."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes that independent replication establishes optical lattice clocks as a general physical method, while requiring historical review of the division between Katori's original concept and Ye's experimental advances.",
        "response": "I agree. Replication is especially probative here because control of trapping and systematic shifts is integral to the scientific result. It shows that the advance is not an isolated laboratory performance. The remaining priority review should be used to keep the citation tightly confined to optical lattice clocks and to test whether the two proposed names capture the essential conceptual and experimental contributions."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The documentary priority record must determine the precise essential roles of Katori and Ye and whether any other contribution has a comparable claim within the narrowly defined optical-lattice-clock achievement.",
      "The final citation should capture the mature transformation of precision measurement without conflating lattice clocks with the wider optical-clock field or overstating still-developing applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile. My work in quantum optics and my WACQT leadership connection to the Gothenburg quantum-technology environment do not identify a collaboration or other direct connection with either proposed laureate."
  }
}
